  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Accenture Federal Services is obligated $2.3B in FY2026 to date across 352 awards, with an average award size of $6.6M. Obligations are down 28.4% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations rose through FY24 and FY25 before declining in FY26: FY21 was $2.5B, FY22 was $2.7B, FY23 was $2.9B, FY24 was $3.4B, FY25 was $3.2B, and FY26 is $2.3B. Computer Systems Design Services is by far the largest market with $2B in obligations, while other IT and professional services markets together account for the remaining work. The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency with $454M, followed by civilian and civilian-adjacent buyers such as the Department of Education with $338M and the Department of Energy with $322M. This profile reflects a heavy government IT and consulting orientation, concentrated in a few large customers and a dominant Computer Systems Design Services market.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What explains the FY2026-to-date decline in obligations?",
        "body": "Accenture Federal Services' obligations are down 28.4% year over year. Fiscal-year totals peaked at $3.4B in FY24 and eased to $3.2B in FY25 before falling to $2.3B in FY26. The drop coincides with a smaller FY26 total compared with the two previous fiscal years. The FY26 figures show fewer or smaller obligations to reach the current $2.3B total."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and markets drive Accenture Federal Services' work?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $454M in obligations, followed by the Department of Education ($338M) and the Department of Energy ($322M). Computer Systems Design Services is the largest market with $2B in obligations, far outpacing Custom Computer Programming Services ($92.6M) and Other Computer Related Services ($90.9M). The top buying agencies and top markets together show a concentration in IT systems design and large civilian and defense customers."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Accenture Federal Services make from federal contracts?",
      "a": "Accenture Federal Services is obligated $2.3B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The company has 352 awards so far in the fiscal year with an average award size of $6.6M, and obligations are down 28.4% year over year."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies buy from Accenture Federal Services?",
      "a": "The top buying agencies in FY2026 to date are the Department of Defense ($454M), the Department of Education ($338M), the Department of Energy ($322M), the Department of Health and Human Services ($234M), and the Department of Veterans Affairs ($199M). Additional significant buyers include the General Services Administration ($181M) and the Department of Homeland Security ($130M)."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Accenture Federal Services sell to the government?",
      "a": "Accenture Federal Services sells IT and professional services, led by Computer Systems Design Services at $2B in obligations. Other significant markets include Custom Computer Programming Services ($92.6M), Other Computer Related Services ($90.9M), and All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services ($40.4M)."
